{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1784881610,"nonce":"Z+E322OqjZ5XvPfOwSZDkStUakg=","title":"ADR-0060 — Org settings surface (`docs/adr/0060-org-settings-connections-and-secret-management.md`).","message":"The manual/UI path to **create a Forgejo `ForgeConnection`**, with credential **write-through**. An \"Add Forgejo connection\" form takes a `base_url` + an access token; the server writes the token into `SecretProvider` under a **server-generated `credential_ref` in the reserved `_forge` org**, stores a DB connection row referencing it, and never echoes the secret back. Also add connection **delete**.\n\nThe token field is write-only: on edit it shows \"•••• set\", never the value. The `_forge` pseudo-org stays an internal mechanism — it is never surfaced to the user. This is the first `POST /v1/connections` create path (GitHub still has none — it auto-registers via webhook).\n\n\n- [ ] `POST /v1/connections` creates a Forgejo connection: persists `{kind, base_url, credential_ref}` and write-through-stores the token as a `_forge`-org secret at the generated ref.\n- [ ] `DELETE /v1/connections/{id}` removes the connection (and is safe w.r.t. its credential secret).\n- [ ] The token is never returned by any endpoint; edit view shows a \"set\" indicator only.\n- [ ] Both endpoints are `Administer`-gated; OpenAPI + UI client regenerated.\n- [ ] \"Add Forgejo connection\" form creates a connection that then appears in the Connections list (with no repos yet).\n- [ ] Functional test: create → connection persisted + credential resolvable via the same `connection_credential()` path; token absent from responses.\n\n\n- 998f20b (Slice 3: connections read API + Connections UI).","files":null}]}